<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Military Frontier</h1><p class="st-meta">1553 CE – 1881 CE · capital Vienna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Habsburg defensive belt along the Ottoman border, where soldier-settlers held land in return for military service and lived under army rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Mohács</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> organised the Croatian and Slavonian border into fortified captaincies, formalised around 1553 with funds from the Austrian estates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlovac">Karlovac</a> was founded in 1579 as a frontier fortress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a> refugees from Ottoman lands were settled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenzer">Grenzer</a>, receiving land and freedom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in return for service.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The frontier was run by army officers answering to the War Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, not to the Croatian ban or Hungarian diet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenzer">Grenzer</a> families lived in communal households, zadrugas, and owed lifelong military service. The 1630 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuta-valachorum">Statuta Valachorum</a> confirmed their privileges. In the 18th century the system was extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a>, guarding against plague as well as raids.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the Ottoman threat gone, the frontier was dissolved between 1871 and 1881 and reattached to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Croatia-Slavonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. It left large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serb</a> communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krajina">Krajina</a> later became the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-serbian-krajina">Republic of Serbian Krajina</a> in the 1990s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/military-frontier.jpg" alt="Military Frontier" width="320" height="111"><figcaption>Image: Scan plp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Militargrenze,_Wojwodowena_und_Banat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1553 CE – 1881 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vienna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian, Serbian, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vojna Krajina, Militärgrenze</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1717&amp;focus=military-frontier" aria-label="Military Frontier on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5073 571 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Military Frontier lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 747h0M5381 747h0M5380 747h0M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5393 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5393 827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Croatia. <a href="/atlas?year=1717&amp;focus=military-frontier">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
